<speak>
From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your daily briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">16022026</say-as>.
<break time="1s"/>
In politics.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Iran</emphasis> sends its foreign minister to Geneva for a second round of indirect nuclear talks with the United States on Tuesday. Both sides remain far apart, with Washington demanding zero enrichment while Tehran offers to dilute its sixty percent uranium stockpile to twenty percent.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Israel</emphasis> sees Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu set four maximalist conditions before those Geneva talks, demanding destruction of all Iranian enrichment equipment and ballistic missiles. President Donald Trump told Netanyahu to let diplomacy continue, exposing a rift between the allies over whether talks can succeed.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Germany</emphasis> and the United Kingdom published a rare joint letter from their top generals warning that Russia's military posture has shifted decisively westward. The unprecedented public call for European rearmament comes as Britain builds six new munitions factories and Germany stations combat brigades on NATO's eastern flank.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Iran</emphasis> heard anti-government chants from balconies across Tehran, Shiraz, and Arak on Sunday night. The protests came one day after diaspora rallies drew a quarter million people in Munich alone, the largest opposition turnout since a January crackdown that killed thousands.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Hungary</emphasis> faces its strongest opposition challenge in sixteen years as Peter Magyar's Tisza party opened its campaign with a double-digit poll lead. The former ruling-party insider who broke with Prime Minister Viktor Orbán pledges to fight corruption and unlock twenty-two point five billion euros in frozen European Union funds.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Tunisia</emphasis> detained opposition leader Olfa Hamdi at Tunis-Carthage airport on Sunday after she demanded early elections to replace President Kais Saied. Rights groups warn Saied has closed more political space than any leader since Tunisia became the only democracy to emerge from the two thousand eleven Arab Spring.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Israel</emphasis> saw hundreds of ultra-Orthodox men chase two female soldiers through <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="bəˈneɪ ˈbɹæk">Bnei Brak</phoneme>, flip a police car, and injure three officers during a routine conscription home visit. The worst violence yet over a June two thousand twenty-four Supreme Court ruling that ended decades of military exemptions deepens the standoff as the military faces manpower shortages.
<break time="900ms"/>
In conflict and security.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Ukraine</emphasis> launched two hundred twenty drones in a single night against Russian energy infrastructure as negotiators departed for a third round of Geneva talks. President Volodymyr <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zeˈlɛnski">Zelenskyy</phoneme> urged serious and substantive negotiations, but Russian forces claim twelve settlements captured this month and the two sides remain far apart on occupied territory.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Lebanon</emphasis> saw an Israeli airstrike kill four people near the Syrian border at <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈmædʒdæl ˈændʒɑɹ">Majdal Anjar</phoneme> early Monday. Israel claimed it targeted Palestinian Islamic Jihad members — the first strike against the group in Lebanon since the November two thousand twenty-four ceasefire with Hezbollah — threatening the truce that ended last year's war.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Iran</emphasis> began naval drills in the Strait of Hormuz carrying twenty percent of the world's traded oil. The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps exercises came one day after the United States deployed a carrier group to the Persian Gulf, with both sides demonstrating leverage ahead of Tuesday's Geneva nuclear talks.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">East Timor</emphasis> saw Myanmar expel its senior diplomat this week after prosecutors accepted a universal jurisdiction complaint naming Myanmar military leader <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="mɪn ɔŋ ˈhlʌɪŋ">Min Aung Hlaing</phoneme>. The charges documenting massacres and sexual violence mark the first time one Association of Southeast Asian Nations member has pursued war crimes against another, just four months after East Timor joined the bloc.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">North Korea</emphasis> inaugurated housing for families of soldiers killed fighting alongside Russian forces in Ukraine. Kim Jong Un's dedication of Saeppyol Street marks the government's most explicit acknowledgment yet of combat losses from its troop deployment.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Sudan</emphasis> courts convicted two women to death by stoning under the nineteen ninety-one Criminal Act, prompting the European Union to warn of a dangerous return to extremism. Rights lawyers report thousands more detainees held in secret across army and Rapid Support Forces-controlled prisons as the country's displacement crisis surpasses eleven million people.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Australia</emphasis> saw the suspect in December's Bondi Beach shooting appear in court facing fifty-nine charges including fifteen murder counts. Twenty-four-year-old <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="nəˈviːd ˈʌkɹəm">Naveed Akram</phoneme>'s lawyer said it was too early to enter a plea for the Hanukkah celebration attack that killed fifteen and wounded forty.
<break time="900ms"/>
On the economy.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Japan</emphasis> reported growth of just zero point one percent last quarter, reversing a prior contraction but falling far short of the one point six percent economists expected. The underwhelming rebound pressures the Bank of Japan to delay further interest rate increases as the world's fourth-largest economy stalls.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Colombia</emphasis> saw its Council of State suspend President Gustavo Petro's twenty-three point seven percent minimum wage decree. The court ruled the government failed to justify the increase and gave the executive eight days to produce a replacement with stronger economic reasoning.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Zambia</emphasis> attracted twelve billion dollars in mining investment since two thousand twenty-two as Africa's second-largest copper producer raised output eight percent last year. Rising demand from electric vehicles and data centres turns the metal into a geopolitical prize, with the United States launching a critical-minerals initiative while China holds stakes in Zambia's largest mines.
<break time="900ms"/>
In science.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">Germany</emphasis> lost a top neuroscientist to China as Arthur <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈkɔnəɹθ">Konnerth</phoneme>, winner of the two thousand fifteen Brain Prize, resigned from the Technical University of Munich to join Shenzhen Bay Laboratory. The highest-profile scientific departure from Europe to China signals deepening talent competition as Beijing builds research institutions in fields overlapping with strategic technologies like artificial intelligence.
<break time="900ms"/>
In technology.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">China</emphasis> saw Alibaba launch <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="tʃwɛn">Qwen</phoneme>-three point five, a three hundred ninety-seven billion parameter model offering multimodal support for text, images, and video. The free-to-download model matches OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google DeepMind while costing sixty percent less to run, accelerating an open-source strategy that undercuts the closed subscription-based approach of American competitors.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">India</emphasis> opened a five-day artificial intelligence summit in New Delhi with Prime Minister Narendra Modi alongside twenty heads of state and tech executives including Google's Sundar <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="pɪˈtʃaɪ">Pichai</phoneme> and OpenAI's Sam Altman. The United Kingdom announced a fifty-eight million pound programme including an African Language Hub to bring capabilities to forty languages spoken by seven hundred million people.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="strong">The United Kingdom</emphasis> announced plans to ban under-sixteens from social media within months by amending existing child-protection legislation. Prime Minister Keir Starmer will separately force all artificial intelligence chatbot providers to comply with the Online Safety Act after non-consensual intimate images appeared on the X platform.
<break time="1s"/>
That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.
</speak>